<Base
  title="Licenses"
  description="What you may do with this site: the writing and images are mine, all rights reserved. The code that builds the site is free to reuse under the MIT license."
  track="plain"
  counterpart="/technical/how-this-site-is-built/#licenses"
>
  <h1>What You May Do With This Site</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    There are two kinds of things here, and they're licensed differently on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>The Writing And Images</h2>
  <p>
    The stories, case studies, timelines, and pictures are mine. All rights reserved. You're welcome to read
    them, link to them, and quote a short passage with credit. Please don't republish them as a whole, or
    pass them off as yours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why keep them closed? It's my voice and my experience, and for now I'd rather not have to keep track of
    other people's licensed material inside an open license of my own. I may open them up later. Opening
    something up can't be undone, so I'm starting closed.
  </p>

  <h2>The Code That Builds The Site</h2>
  <p>
    The site's source code is under the MIT license. That's a short, permissive software license: anyone can
    copy, change, and reuse the code, for anything, as long as they keep the copyright notice and license
    text with it. It comes with no warranty.
  </p>
  <p>
    The code is the reusable, ordinary part: layouts, tests, the build. If any of it helps someone build their
    own site, good.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Different Licenses</h2>
  <p>
    Software licenses are written for code. Applied to writing, MIT would let anyone republish the essays with
    only the notice kept. Splitting them is common: code under a software license, content under its own terms.
  </p>

  <p class="panel">
    The exact legal text of the code's license is in the site's repository:{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/spirrochet/spirrochet.github.io/blob/main/LICENSE">the MIT license</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
